<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<model version="NetLogo 7.0.4" snapToGrid="false">
  <code><![CDATA[;; =====================================================================
;; POLITICAL COMPROMISE MODEL  (DC Seminar, agent-based model, NetLogo 7)
;; =====================================================================
;;
;; PURPOSE
;;   Predict how often a small legislature passes a compromise bill over a
;;   series of votes. Two factors drive it:
;;     (1) how extreme each legislator's constituency is, and
;;     (2) how legislators learn from the results of past votes
;;         (imitation dynamics, reinforcement learning, or both).
;;
;; LEVELS FROM THE ORIGINAL OUTLINE -> SETTINGS IN THIS FILE
;;   All levels live in this one model, chosen with two settings,
;;   instead of separate files:
;;     Level 0-1 : learning-mode = "Imitation",     num-agents = 6
;;     Level 0-2 : learning-mode = "Reinforcement", num-agents = 6
;;     Level 0.5 : Imitation or Reinforcement,      num-agents = 30
;;     Level 1   : learning-mode = "Combined",      num-agents = 30
;;
;; THE BILLS
;;   Every trial has three bills: RED, BLUE and PURPLE. Purple is a single
;;   bill that already contains concessions from both parties. Compromise
;;   means "purple got the most votes". It does NOT mean that a particular
;;   red legislator and blue legislator agreed with each other. That is why
;;   an earlier version's lines drawing "compromise links" between agents
;;   were removed. As the outline specifies, agents only ever vote for their
;;   own party's bill or for purple, never for the other party's bill.
;;
;; SPACE AND TIME (from the outline)
;;   - Agents sit still in a 2-D view. Location has no meaning beyond
;;     showing red on the left and blue on the right.
;;   - Time is counted in trials, not clock time. Each tick is one new bill.
;;   - Agents act synchronously: everyone votes, then everyone learns.
;;   - The run stops after the number of trials the user chooses.
;;
;; DESIGN DECISIONS MADE WITH THE MODEL'S AUTHOR WHILE BUILDING IT
;;   1. Platform: NetLogo, one file with a mode switch (see levels above).
;;   2. Vote rule: plurality. The bill with the most votes wins, and a tie
;;      for first place is a "tie", which pays everyone 0.
;;   3. Imitation signal: u_a = the average payoff of ALL agents in the
;;      chamber who chose action a, pooling both parties. Known side effect,
;;      which the author reviewed and chose to keep: when red's bill wins,
;;      red holdouts (5) and blue holdouts (1) are averaged together, so
;;      holding out can look good to blue agents even though blue lost.
;;   4. Imitation equation: the outline's literal logit is the DEFAULT.
;;      Because every agent sees the same u values, this makes all agents
;;      identical after trial 1, so extremism only shapes the first vote.
;;      The keep-own-tendency? switch (off by default) is an optional
;;      variant that keeps agents different from each other.
;;   5. When there is nothing to compare (everyone made the same choice,
;;      or the vote tied), imitation leaves probabilities unchanged.
;;   6. Compromise success rate = purple wins / trials run.
;;   7. Combined mode: P(use imitation) = 1 - extremism, so agents from
;;      more extreme districts rely more on reinforcement. Imitation and
;;      reinforcement are deliberately NOT synchronized. An imitation
;;      change lasts until that agent's next reinforcement step, which
;;      recomputes P from its own propensities. Reinforcement acts as
;;      long-term memory. The author chose this over syncing the two.
;;   8. Reinforcement uses forgetting (recency), fixed at 0.1 per trial,
;;      roughly the value Erev & Roth (1998) estimated from human
;;      experiments. It is a constant in the code, not a slider.
;;   9. The payoff table is fixed in the code, with no payoff sliders.
;;  10. Lambda (imitation sensitivity) is a slider from 0 to 1 (step 0.05,
;;      default 1). It was originally 0-5. The logit itself allows any
;;      lambda >= 0, but the author chose 0-1. With payoffs of 0-5 this
;;      still spans "no response" (0) to strong copying (1 = 88% chance
;;      of the better action for a 2-point payoff gap).
;;  11. Extremism input:
;;        6 agents  -> one slider per agent, used exactly.
;;        30 agents -> 5 groups of 3 per party. The user picks a bucket
;;                     (0-20, 20-40, 40-60, 60-80 or 80-100%) and each
;;                     agent's value is drawn at random within it.
;;      NetLogo cannot show or hide widgets based on another setting, so
;;      both sets of controls are always visible, under labeled headers.
;;  12. Visuals: agents are circles on a plain white background, colored
;;      by the bill they just voted for. Each label always shows that
;;      agent's P(compromise) for the next trial. The votes bar chart uses
;;      solid bars.
;;
;; BUG FIXES ADDED DURING DEBUGGING
;;   A. All settings are locked in at setup, so moving a widget mid-run does
;;      nothing until the next setup. The outline says parameters change
;;      only on reset.
;;   B. A tied vote gives imitation no signal. A tie pays everyone 0, and
;;      without this rule the literal logit would reset all agents to 50/50.
;;   C. P(compromise) is always kept between 1% and 99%, including at the
;;      start. Without this limit, learning pushed agents to within ~1e-12
;;      of 0 or 1, where they could never change again. The author chose
;;      1%-99%. (5%-95% was used for a while, then switched back to 1%-99%.)
;; =====================================================================


;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;; GLOBAL VARIABLES
;; Sliders, choosers and switches on the Interface tab are also globals.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
globals [
  winner                              ; result of latest trial: "red", "blue", "purple" or "tie"
  red-votes blue-votes purple-votes   ; votes cast in the latest trial (bar chart)
  total-red total-blue total-purple   ; cumulative votes over all trials (purple-vote-share monitor)
  red-wins blue-wins purple-wins ties ; cumulative outcomes (purple-wins drives the success rate)
  u-compromise                        ; avg payoff of ALL agents who voted purple last trial (decision 3)
  u-hold                              ; avg payoff of ALL agents who voted their own party's bill last trial
  imitation-info?                     ; true when imitation has a usable signal this trial (decision 5, fix B)

  ;; Copies of the Interface settings, saved at setup (fix A).
  ;; The rest of the code reads these copies, never the widgets directly.
  run-mode        ; copy of learning-mode
  run-lambda      ; copy of lambda
  run-keep-own?   ; copy of keep-own-tendency?
  run-trials      ; copy of num-trials
]


;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;; AGENT VARIABLES
;; There is one agent type: a legislator. The outline also had no other
;; objects in the model (no institutions or bills as agents).
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
turtles-own [
  party          ; "red" or "blue": fixed for the whole run
  group-id       ; constituency group: 1-3 with 6 agents (one agent each), 1-5 with 30 (3 agents each)
  extremism      ; constituency extremism, 0-1. Set once at setup and never changes.
  p-compromise   ; current probability of voting purple. This is what learning changes.
  choice         ; vote this trial: "purple", or the agent's own party ("red"/"blue")
  payoff         ; reward this trial, from the payoff table
  q-compromise   ; reinforcement propensity ("accumulated experience") for voting purple
  q-hold         ; reinforcement propensity for voting own party's bill
  p-imitate      ; Combined mode only: chance of using imitation this trial (= 1 - extremism, decision 7)
  method         ; learning rule actually used this trial: "imitation" or "reinforcement"
]


;; =====================================================================
;; SETUP: build the chamber and give every agent its starting state
;; =====================================================================

to setup
  clear-all
  set-default-shape turtles "circle"    ; decision 12: agents are circles
  set winner "none"
  set imitation-info? false

  ;; Fix A: lock in settings now. Changing them mid-run has no effect
  ;; until the next setup, matching "users must reset to change parameters".
  set run-mode learning-mode
  set run-lambda lambda
  set run-keep-own? keep-own-tendency?
  set run-trials num-trials

  draw-chamber
  make-agents "red"
  make-agents "blue"
  reset-ticks
  do-plots
end

;; Decision 12: plain white background. The earlier red/purple/blue
;; background bands were removed at the author's request.
to draw-chamber
  ask patches [ set pcolor white ]
end

;; Creates one party's legislators: 3 with 6 agents, 15 with 30 agents.
;; The outline splits agents evenly between the two parties.
to make-agents [ pty ]
  let per-party num-agents / 2
  let i 0
  repeat per-party [
    create-turtles 1 [
      set party pty

      ;; ---- constituency extremism (decision 11) ----
      ifelse num-agents = 6 [
        ;; Level 0: each of the 3 per-party sliders sets one agent exactly.
        set group-id i + 1
        set extremism base-extremism pty group-id
      ] [
        ;; Levels 0.5 / 1: each party has 5 groups of 3. Each group shares
        ;; a bucket, and each agent's exact value is random within it.
        ;; This replaced the outline's original "+/- 5%" spread.
        set group-id floor (i / 3) + 1
        set extremism bucket-extremism pty group-id
      ]

      ;; ---- starting willingness to compromise ----
      ;; From the outline: willingness = the inverse of extremism. A 70%
      ;; extreme district gives a 30% chance of voting purple. Fix C keeps
      ;; it within 1%-99%.
      set p-compromise bound-p (1 - extremism)

      ;; ---- reinforcement starting propensities (Level 0-2 / Combined) ----
      ;; Split initial-propensity in the same ratio as p-compromise, so an
      ;; agent's first reinforcement-based P matches its constituency.
      ;; A larger initial-propensity means more "prior experience", so each
      ;; new payoff moves the agent less (slower learning).
      set q-compromise initial-propensity * p-compromise
      set q-hold initial-propensity * (1 - p-compromise)

      ;; ---- Combined mode (Level 1, decision 7) ----
      ;; More extreme districts -> more reliance on reinforcement learning,
      ;; which the outline describes as more prone to feedback loops.
      set p-imitate 1 - extremism

      set choice ""
      set method ""
      set heading 0
      place-agent i
      set color ifelse-value (party = "red") [ red ] [ blue ]   ; party color until the first vote
      set label-color black
      update-label
    ]
    set i i + 1
  ]
end

;; Places agents in fixed seats: red on the left, blue on the right.
;; Position is only for display and plays no part in the model (outline).
to place-agent [ i ]
  let side ifelse-value (party = "red") [ -1 ] [ 1 ]
  ifelse num-agents = 6 [
    set size 3
    setxy (side * 9) (6 - 6 * i)            ; one column of 3 per party
  ] [
    set size 2
    let col i mod 3                          ; 3 agents per row = one constituency group
    let row floor (i / 3)                    ; 5 rows = 5 groups
    setxy (side * (8 + 3 * col)) (12 - 6 * row)
  ]
end

;; 6-agent mode: reads the exact extremism slider for agent g (1-3).
to-report base-extremism [ pty g ]
  let sliders ifelse-value (pty = "red")
    [ (list red-agent-1 red-agent-2 red-agent-3) ]
    [ (list blue-agent-1 blue-agent-2 blue-agent-3) ]
  report (item (g - 1) sliders) / 100
end

;; The five bucket choices, in order. A chooser's position in this list
;; (0-4) gives the bucket's lower edge in steps of 20%.
to-report bucket-names
  report [ "0-20%" "20-40%" "40-60%" "60-80%" "80-100%" ]
end

;; 30-agent mode: random extremism inside group g's chosen bucket,
;; e.g. "60-80%" gives a uniform random value from 0.60 up to (not including) 0.80.
to-report bucket-extremism [ pty g ]
  let buckets ifelse-value (pty = "red")
    [ (list red-group-1 red-group-2 red-group-3 red-group-4 red-group-5) ]
    [ (list blue-group-1 blue-group-2 blue-group-3 blue-group-4 blue-group-5) ]
  let b position (item (g - 1) buckets) bucket-names
  report (20 * b + random-float 20) / 100
end

;; Fix C: every legislator always keeps at least a 1% chance of breaking
;; from their usual vote. Without this, learning drives P(compromise) to
;; ~0 or ~1 and agents lock in permanently. The author chose 1%-99%.
to-report p-floor
  report 0.01
end

;; Clamps any probability into [p-floor, 1 - p-floor] = [0.01, 0.99].
to-report bound-p [ p ]
  report max (list p-floor (min (list (1 - p-floor) p)))
end


;; =====================================================================
;; MAIN LOOP: one tick = one trial = one bill voted on
;; Order each trial: vote -> count -> pay -> learn -> redraw -> plot.
;; Agents act synchronously: everyone votes before anyone learns.
;; =====================================================================

to go
  if ticks >= run-trials [ stop ]   ; stop after the user-chosen number of trials
  vote
  tally
  pay
  learn
  visualize
  tick
  do-plots
end

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;; VOTE: each agent votes purple with probability p-compromise and
;; otherwise votes for its own party's bill. On trial 1, p-compromise comes
;; straight from extremism. Later it is whatever learning produced.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
to vote
  ask turtles [
    set choice ifelse-value (random-float 1 < p-compromise) [ "purple" ] [ party ]
  ]
end

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;; TALLY: count votes and pick the winner (decision 2: plurality rule).
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
to tally
  set red-votes    count turtles with [ choice = "red" ]
  set blue-votes   count turtles with [ choice = "blue" ]
  set purple-votes count turtles with [ choice = "purple" ]
  set total-red    total-red + red-votes
  set total-blue   total-blue + blue-votes
  set total-purple total-purple + purple-votes

  ;; Most votes wins. If two or more bills share the top count, the trial
  ;; is a "tie". With 6 agents this is common, e.g. 2-2-2 or 3-3-0.
  let counts (list red-votes blue-votes purple-votes)
  let top max counts
  ifelse length filter [ c -> c = top ] counts > 1 [
    set winner "tie"
    set ties ties + 1
  ] [
    set winner item (position top counts) [ "red" "blue" "purple" ]
    if winner = "red"    [ set red-wins red-wins + 1 ]
    if winner = "blue"   [ set blue-wins blue-wins + 1 ]
    if winner = "purple" [ set purple-wins purple-wins + 1 ]
  ]
end

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;; PAY: every agent gets a reward from the outline's payoff table, based
;; on its own vote and on which bill won.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
to pay
  ask turtles [ set payoff payoff-for choice party winner ]
end

;; Payoff table from the outline (decision 9: fixed, not adjustable)
;;                     own bill wins | purple wins | own bill loses | tie
;;  compromise               3       |      4      |       0        |  0
;;  don't compromise         5       |      2      |       1        |  0
;; "Own bill" means the agent's party's bill. "Loses" means the OTHER
;; party's bill won.
to-report payoff-for [ ch pty win ]
  if win = "tie" [ report 0 ]
  ifelse ch = "purple" [
    if win = "purple" [ report 4 ]   ; voted for compromise and it passed
    if win = pty      [ report 3 ]   ; compromised, but own party's bill passed anyway
    report 0                         ; compromised, and the other party's bill passed
  ] [
    if win = pty      [ report 5 ]   ; held out and own party's bill passed (best case)
    if win = "purple" [ report 2 ]   ; held out but compromise passed
    report 1                         ; held out and the other party's bill passed
  ]
end


;; =====================================================================
;; LEARNING: update each agent's p-compromise for the NEXT trial
;; =====================================================================

to learn
  ;; ---- imitation signal (decision 3) ----
  ;; Average payoff for each action across the WHOLE chamber, both parties
  ;; pooled. Every agent sees the same two numbers.
  let comps turtles with [ choice = "purple" ]
  let holds turtles with [ choice != "purple" ]

  ;; Decision 5 and fix B: there is no signal if nobody (or everybody)
  ;; compromised, or if the vote tied. A tie pays everyone 0, which would
  ;; otherwise reset everyone to 50/50.
  set imitation-info? (any? comps and any? holds and winner != "tie")
  if imitation-info? [
    set u-compromise mean [ payoff ] of comps
    set u-hold mean [ payoff ] of holds
  ]

  ask turtles [
    ;; Propensities are updated EVERY trial in every mode, so reinforcement
    ;; memory stays current even for agents that used imitation this trial.
    reinforce-accumulate

    ;; Pick this trial's learning rule
    if run-mode = "Imitation"     [ set method "imitation" ]       ; Level 0-1
    if run-mode = "Reinforcement" [ set method "reinforcement" ]   ; Level 0-2
    if run-mode = "Combined" [                                      ; Level 1, decision 7
      set method ifelse-value (random-float 1 < p-imitate) [ "imitation" ] [ "reinforcement" ]
    ]
    ifelse method = "imitation" [ imitate ] [ reinforce ]
  ]
end

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;; IMITATION DYNAMICS (Level 0-1). The outline's logit equation:
;;     P(a) = e^(lambda * u_a) / sum over q of e^(lambda * u_q)
;; with a in {compromise, hold}.
;;   lambda = how strongly agents respond to payoff differences.
;;            0 -> payoffs ignored (literal form gives everyone 50/50).
;;            Higher -> a small payoff edge pulls nearly everyone to the
;;            better action. Because payoffs run 0-5, lambda = 1
;;            (the slider maximum) is already a strong response.
;;
;; keep-own-tendency? OFF (DEFAULT, decision 4): the literal equation.
;;   Every agent gets the SAME probability after trial 1, so extremism
;;   only matters for the first vote. In testing, the first vote then
;;   largely decided the whole run: purple won ~0% or ~100% of the time.
;; keep-own-tendency? ON (optional variant): each term is weighted by the
;;   agent's current probability,  P'(a) ∝ P(a) * e^(lambda * u_a).
;;   lambda = 0 then means "no change", and agents keep their
;;   constituency-driven differences.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
to imitate
  if imitation-info? [
    let wc exp (run-lambda * u-compromise)   ; weight on compromising
    let wh exp (run-lambda * u-hold)         ; weight on holding out
    ifelse run-keep-own? [
      set p-compromise (p-compromise * wc) / (p-compromise * wc + (1 - p-compromise) * wh)
    ] [
      set p-compromise wc / (wc + wh)
    ]
    set p-compromise bound-p p-compromise    ; fix C: stay within 1%-99%
  ]
  ;; With no signal, p-compromise is left unchanged (decision 5).
end

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;; REINFORCEMENT LEARNING (Level 0-2). The outline's equation is
;;     p_k(t) = q_k(t) / sum over j of q_j(t)
;; (Roth-Erev). Each agent learns only from its OWN payoffs, not from
;; others. With forgetting (decision 8), each trial:
;;     q_k <- (1 - forgetting) * q_k + payoff   if k was chosen
;;     q_k <- (1 - forgetting) * q_k            otherwise
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

;; Decision 8: fixed recency rate. With 0.1, a payoff from 10 trials ago
;; counts about 35% as much as the latest one (0.9^10 ≈ 0.35). It is a
;; constant here, not a slider, at the author's request.
to-report forgetting
  report 0.1
end

;; Decays old experience, then adds this trial's payoff to the action taken.
;; Runs every trial for every agent (see learn).
to reinforce-accumulate
  set q-compromise (1 - forgetting) * q-compromise
  set q-hold (1 - forgetting) * q-hold
  ifelse choice = "purple"
    [ set q-compromise q-compromise + payoff ]
    [ set q-hold q-hold + payoff ]
end

;; Turns propensities into a probability, kept within 1%-99% (fix C).
;; Decision 7: this recomputes P from the agent's own propensities alone,
;; so it overrides any earlier imitation change. That is intentional:
;; reinforcement is the agent's long-term memory. The guard skips the
;; update if both propensities are 0, which would otherwise divide by zero.
to reinforce
  if q-compromise + q-hold > 0 [
    set p-compromise bound-p (q-compromise / (q-compromise + q-hold))
  ]
end


;; =====================================================================
;; VISUALS AND OUTPUTS
;; =====================================================================

;; Decision 12: each circle takes the color of the bill it just voted for
;; (red, blue or purple). No links are drawn, because purple is a single
;; compromise bill, not an agreement between specific agents.
to visualize
  ask turtles [
    set color ifelse-value (choice = "purple") [ violet ] [ ifelse-value (party = "red") [ red ] [ blue ] ]
    update-label
  ]
end

;; Decision 12: the label always shows P(compromise) for the NEXT trial.
;; The earlier on/off label switch was removed.
to update-label
  set label precision p-compromise 2
end

;; ---- outputs listed in the outline ----

;; Decision 6: compromise success rate = purple bills passed / trials run.
;; For example, 0.8 means purple won 80% of the votes so far.
to-report compromise-success-rate
  report ifelse-value (ticks > 0) [ purple-wins / ticks ] [ 0 ]
end

;; Extra indicator: share of ALL individual votes that went to purple.
;; This differs from the success rate: purple can win often with modest
;; vote share, because it draws votes from both parties.
to-report purple-vote-share
  let total total-red + total-blue + total-purple
  report ifelse-value (total > 0) [ total-purple / total ] [ 0 ]
end

;; Average P(compromise) within one party (Willingness plot and monitors).
;; In literal Imitation mode, red, blue and all-agents averages are
;; identical after trial 1 (decision 4), so the three plot lines overlap.
to-report mean-p [ pty ]
  report mean [ p-compromise ] of turtles with [ party = pty ]
end

;; Monitors use these argument-free versions.
to-report mean-p-red
  report mean-p "red"
end

to-report mean-p-blue
  report mean-p "blue"
end

;; Average constituency extremism of all agents, as a percent. Uses each
;; agent's ACTUAL value: with 30 agents, the random number drawn inside its
;; group's bucket, not the bucket midpoint. With 6 agents it is simply the
;; average of the six sliders. Fixed for the whole run, since extremism
;; never changes after setup. Added at the author's request.
to-report mean-extremism
  report 100 * mean [ extremism ] of turtles
end

;; Share of agents that used imitation (vs reinforcement) on the last trial.
;; Only meaningful in Combined mode. In the other modes it is always 100 or 0.
to-report pct-imitating
  report 100 * count turtles with [ method = "imitation" ] / count turtles
end

;; Draws all four plots from code, not from widget update code, so every
;; plot uses the same trial numbering.
;;   Votes This Trial          - bar chart of the latest vote (outline)
;;   Purple Vote % per Trial   - % of agents voting purple in each trial. This
;;                               replaced the outline's cumulative-votes line chart
;;                               at the author's request, to show each trial on its own
;;                               rather than running totals
;;   Willingness to Compromise - average P(compromise): red, blue, everyone
;;   Compromise Success Rate   - purple wins / trials so far
to do-plots
  set-current-plot "Votes This Trial"
  clear-plot                                ; shows only the latest trial
  set-plot-y-range 0 count turtles
  if ticks > 0 [
    draw-bar "red"    0 red-votes
    draw-bar "purple" 1 purple-votes
    draw-bar "blue"   2 blue-votes
  ]

  set-current-plot "Purple Vote % per Trial"
  if ticks > 0 [   ; nothing to show before the first vote
    set-current-plot-pen "purple %" plotxy ticks 100 * purple-votes / count turtles
  ]

  set-current-plot "Willingness to Compromise"
  set-current-plot-pen "red party"  plotxy ticks mean-p "red"
  set-current-plot-pen "blue party" plotxy ticks mean-p "blue"
  set-current-plot-pen "all agents" plotxy ticks mean [ p-compromise ] of turtles

  set-current-plot "Compromise Success Rate"
  set-current-plot-pen "success rate" plotxy ticks compromise-success-rate
end

;; Decision 12: NetLogo 7 draws bar-mode plots as outlines, so a solid
;; colored bar is built by stacking many 0.01-wide bars side by side,
;; from x0 + 0.1 to x0 + 0.9.
to draw-bar [ pen-name x0 h ]
  set-current-plot-pen pen-name
  set-plot-pen-interval 0.01
  let x x0 + 0.1
  while [ x < x0 + 0.9 ] [
    plotxy x h
    set x x + 0.01
  ]
end
]]></code>
  <widgets>
    <view x="440" wrappingAllowedX="false" y="10" frameRate="30.0" minPycor="-16" height="429" showTickCounter="true" patchSize="13.0" fontSize="10" wrappingAllowedY="false" width="429" tickCounterLabel="trials" maxPycor="16" updateMode="1" maxPxcor="16" minPxcor="-16"></view>
    <button x="5" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="95" display="setup">setup</button>
    <button x="105" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="100" display="one trial">go</button>
    <button x="5" y="55" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="200" display="run all trials">go</button>
    <chooser x="5" y="100" height="60" variable="learning-mode" current="0" width="200" display="learning-mode"><choice type="string" value="Imitation"></choice><choice type="string" value="Reinforcement"></choice><choice type="string" value="Combined"></choice></chooser>
    <chooser x="5" y="165" height="60" variable="num-agents" current="0" width="200" display="num-agents"><choice type="double" value="6.0"></choice><choice type="double" value="30.0"></choice></chooser>
    <slider x="5" step="1" y="230" max="1000" width="200" display="num-trials" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="100" variable="num-trials"></slider>
    <slider x="5" step="0.05" y="285" max="1" width="200" display="lambda" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="lambda"></slider>
    <slider x="5" step="1" y="340" max="50" width="200" display="initial-propensity" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="10" variable="initial-propensity"></slider>
